`aplan deprecations` reads the runways a plan already declares: an announcement
tagged `deprecation`, a removal tagged `removal` naming it in `blocked_by`, and
the cut each one is a member of. Nothing new in the frontmatter.

```
$ aplan deprecations
T-40  Announce that `--legacy-format` is going away
  announced in v0.3 (todo)
  removed in v0.5 by T-51 (todo)

T-52  Remove the undocumented `APLAN_LEGACY` variable
  ! tagged `removal` and blocked by no `deprecation` task: a removal nobody was told about

1 runway(s), 1 removal(s) with nothing announcing them. A runway is the gap between the two cuts; this reports its shape and never fails a build.

```

`--format markdown` is the table a project publishes. The first column is the
announcing task's own title, so write it as the notice you want read.

```
$ aplan deprecations --format markdown
<!-- generated by `aplan deprecations --format markdown`; do not edit. -->

# Deprecations

| Announcement | Announced in | Removed in | Status |
|---|---|---|---|
| Announce that `--legacy-format` is going away | v0.3 | v0.5 | planned |

```

A format this command does not take names the ones it does, rather than every
rendering the tool has.

```
$ aplan deprecations --format sarif
? 2
aplan: `aplan deprecations` renders a document, so it takes --format `human`, `json` or `markdown`, not `sarif`; `sarif` renders a set of findings, which `check` and `verify` do

```
